steps.body:
An interface describes a role, and `implements` asks the checker to compare a class with it. A missing member, or one with the wrong type, is a type error at the class, right where the mistake is:

```typescript
interface Greeter {
  greet(name: string): string;
}

class EnglishGreeter implements Greeter {
  greet(name: string): string {
    return `Hello, ${name}`;
  }
}

const greeter: Greeter = new EnglishGreeter();
console.log(greeter.greet("Ada")); // Hello, Ada
```

`implements` is only a check. It adds no code and no members, and Node erases it. The class still declares every field and method itself, parameter types included, because a class method doesn't take them from the interface. A class may have members the interface doesn't mention, and it may implement several interfaces, separated by commas.

Because TypeScript compares shapes, any value with a matching `greet` is a `Greeter`, whether a class says `implements` or not.

examples.code:
interface Notifier {
  readonly channel: string;
  send(to: string, message: string): string;
}

class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
  readonly channel = "email";
  //       ^? (property) EmailNotifier.channel: "email"
  from: string;

  constructor(from: string) {
    this.from = from;
  }

  send(to: string, message: string): string {
    return `From ${this.from} to ${to}: ${message}`;
  }
}

class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
  readonly channel = "sms";

  send(to: string, message: string): string {
    const text = message.length > 20 ? `${message.slice(0, 17)}...` : message;
    return `To ${to}: ${text}`;
  }
}

const notifiers: Notifier[] = [new EmailNotifier("shop@example.com"), new SmsNotifier()];
for (const notifier of notifiers) {
  console.log(`[${notifier.channel}] ${notifier.send("Ada", "Your order has shipped")}`);
}

interface Notifier {
  send(to: string, message: string): string;
}

class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
  sendText(to: string, message: string): string {
    return `SMS to ${to}: ${message}`;
  }
}

console.log(new SmsNotifier().sendText("Ada", "Your order has shipped"));

examples.explanation:
`SmsNotifier` promises to be a `Notifier`, but its method is called `sendText`, so `tsc` stops at the class name with TS2420 and names the missing `send` on the next line. A method with the right name and the wrong type fails too:

```typescript error
interface Notifier {
  send(to: string, message: string): string;
}

class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
  send(to: string, message: string): boolean {
    return message.length <= 160;
  }
}

console.log(new SmsNotifier().send("Ada", "Hi"));
```

That's TS2416: `Property 'send' in type 'SmsNotifier' is not assignable to the same property in base type 'Notifier'.` Without `implements`, both classes would compile, and the mistake would only show up where one is first used as a `Notifier`, maybe in another file.

examples.code:
interface Notifier {
  send(to: string, message: string): string;
}

class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
  send(to: string, message: string): string {
    return `Email to ${to}: ${message}`;
  }
}

function remind(notifier: Notifier, names: string[]): void {
  for (const name of names) {
    console.log(notifier.send(name, "Your trial ends tomorrow"));
  }
}

const devNotifier: Notifier = {
  send: (to, message) => `[dev] ${to} <- ${message}`,
};

remind(new EmailNotifier(), ["Ada"]);
remind(devNotifier, ["Ben", "Caro"]);

examples.explanation:
`remind` asks for a `Notifier`, and both arguments fit. `devNotifier` is a plain object with no class at all, but it has the right `send`, and structural typing only compares shapes. Its arrow function needs no parameter types, because the `Notifier` annotation supplies them, which a class method never gets. An interface exists only for the checker, so you can't test for it at runtime:

```typescript error
interface Notifier {
  send(to: string, message: string): string;
}

const devNotifier: Notifier = {
  send: (to, message) => `[dev] ${to} <- ${message}`,
};

console.log(devNotifier instanceof Notifier);
```

That's TS2693: `'Notifier' only refers to a type, but is being used as a value here.` To tell implementations apart at runtime, use `instanceof` with a class, or give the interface a tag field, as discriminated unions do.

