practice.solution:
export type Row = { sku: string; name: string; qty: number };

export interface Exporter {
  readonly extension: string;
  export(rows: readonly Row[]): string;
}

export class CsvExporter implements Exporter {
  readonly extension = "csv";

  export(rows: readonly Row[]): string {
    const lines = rows.map((row) => `${row.sku},${row.name},${row.qty}`);
    return ["sku,name,qty", ...lines].join("\n");
  }
}

export class JsonExporter implements Exporter {
  readonly extension = "json";

  export(rows: readonly Row[]): string {
    return JSON.stringify(rows);
  }
}

const rows: Row[] = [
  { sku: "MUG-01", name: "Mug", qty: 3 },
  { sku: "TEA-02", name: "Green tea", qty: 12 },
];

const exporters: Exporter[] = [new CsvExporter(), new JsonExporter()];
for (const exporter of exporters) {
  console.log(`--- stock.${exporter.extension}`);
  console.log(exporter.export(rows));
}

tests.append:
const more: Row[] = [{ sku: "PEN-07", name: "Pen", qty: 40 }];
console.log(new CsvExporter().export(more));
console.log(new JsonExporter().export(more));
console.log(new CsvExporter().export([]));

steps.body:
An abstract class is a base class that is only partly written, on purpose. It holds the code its subclasses share, and it declares with `abstract` the members each subclass must write:

```typescript
abstract class Greeting {
  abstract text(): string;

  shout(): string {
    return `${this.text().toUpperCase()}!`;
  }
}

class Hello extends Greeting {
  text(): string {
    return "hello";
  }
}

console.log(new Hello().shout()); // HELLO!
```

An abstract member has a type and no body. You can't create the abstract class itself (TS2511), and a subclass that leaves out an abstract member is TS2515, unless it's abstract too. A subclass that fills in an abstract member doesn't need `override`, since there's no base code to replace, though you may write it. Its version may also leave off parameters at the end that it doesn't use.

The `abstract` keyword is a rule for the checker, and Node erases it. What's left is an ordinary JavaScript class, so subclasses inherit its fields and methods at runtime, while an interface leaves nothing behind. Use an interface to describe a role, and an abstract class when the implementations share code.

examples.code:
abstract class Report {
  title: string;

  constructor(title: string) {
    this.title = title;
  }

  abstract rows(): string[];

  render(): string {
    const lines = this.rows().map((row) => `- ${row}`);
    return [`# ${this.title}`, ...lines, `(${lines.length} rows)`].join("\n");
  }
}

class LowStockReport extends Report {
  stock: Record<string, number>;

  constructor(stock: Record<string, number>) {
    super("Low stock");
    this.stock = stock;
  }

  rows(): string[] {
    return Object.entries(this.stock)
      .filter(([, qty]) => qty < 5)
      .map(([sku, qty]) => `${sku}: ${qty} left`);
  }
}

class SignupReport extends Report {
  days: [day: string, count: number][];

  constructor(days: [day: string, count: number][]) {
    super("Signups");
    this.days = days;
  }

  rows(): string[] {
    return this.days.map(([day, count]) => `${day}: ${count}`);
  }
}

const reports: Report[] = [
  new LowStockReport({ MUG: 2, TEA: 40, PEN: 0 }),
  new SignupReport([["Mon", 12], ["Tue", 9], ["Wed", 15]]),
];
for (const report of reports) {
  console.log(report.render());
}

abstract class Report {
  abstract rows(): string[];

  render(): string {
    return this.rows().join("\n");
  }
}

class DraftReport extends Report {}

const report = new Report();
console.log(report.render());

examples.explanation:
Two rules hold an abstract class together. `DraftReport` extends `Report` without writing `rows()`, so `tsc` reports TS2515 at the class: it would inherit a `render()` that calls a method it doesn't have. And `new Report()` is TS2511, for the same reason. Both checks belong to the compiler. With checking turned off, Node creates the object, and the crash comes later, when `render()` runs:

```typescript error
// @ts-nocheck
abstract class Report {
  abstract rows(): string[];

  render(): string {
    return this.rows().join("\n");
  }
}

const report = new Report();
console.log(report.render());
```

That run fails with `TypeError: this.rows is not a function`.

examples.code:
abstract class Validator {
  readonly field: string;
  errors: string[] = [];

  constructor(field: string) {
    this.field = field;
  }

  abstract check(value: string): void;

  protected fail(message: string): void {
    this.errors.push(`${this.field} ${message}`);
  }
}

class RequiredValidator extends Validator {
  check(value: string): void {
    if (value.trim() === "") {
      this.fail("is required");
    }
  }
}

class EmailValidator extends Validator {
  check(value: string): void {
    if (!value.includes("@")) {
      this.fail("must contain @");
    }
    if (value !== value.toLowerCase()) {
      this.fail("must be lowercase");
    }
  }
}

const form: Record<string, string> = { name: " ", email: "Ada.Lovelace" };
const validators: Validator[] = [new RequiredValidator("name"), new EmailValidator("email")];
for (const validator of validators) {
  validator.check(form[validator.field] ?? "");
  for (const error of validator.errors) {
    console.log(error);
  }
}

